CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF NORTH GORONTALO

Considering:
a. that in order to protect the constitutional rights of women and children in accordance with the mandate of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, the Government of North Gorontalo Regency is responsible for the implementation of protection of women and children against acts of violence.
b. that North Gorontalo Regency which has the Philosophy of Custom Based on Sharia, Sharia Based on the Book of Allah, upholds Religious Values and Customs as well as noble Character, the Government of North Gorontalo Regency is morally fully responsible for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.  c. that every citizen has the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades dignity, self-worth and human dignity and has the right to obtain security and freedom from all forms of violence d. that the increase in violent treatment against women and children in North Gorontalo Regency is an act that degrades human dignity, so that the role of the Government of North Gorontalo Regency is required so that women and children are protected from acts of violence e. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children Against Acts of Violence Bearing in Mind:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
3. Law Number 38 of 2000 concerning the Establishment of Gorontalo Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 182, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4010);
4. Law Number 11 of 2007 concerning the establishment of North Gorontalo Regency;
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
6. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
7. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
8. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
9. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
10. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning Division of Government Affairs between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 437);
11. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Women's Protection;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;
18. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products;

With Mutual Approval

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence is implemented based on the principles of:

a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of Victims of Acts of Violence;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. for victims of acts of violence; and
e. legal certainty.

Article 5

Protection of women as referred to in Article 4 letter a includes:

a. prevention of acts of violence;
b. integrated services for victims of acts of violence;
c. empowerment of victims of acts of violence.

Article 7

The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:

a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and
h. threats of certain actions.

Article 8

Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of violence as referred to in Article 7 is obliged to make efforts to:

a. prevent the continuation of criminal acts;
b. provide protection to victims of acts of violence;
c. provide emergency assistance; and/or
d. assist in the process of submitting an application for protection determination

Article 9

1. The Regional Government carries out prevention as referred to in Article 5 letter a by:

a. socializing laws and regulations;
b. providing counseling/guidance;
c. providing education on the dangers of domestic violence;
d. conducting seminars/workshops or the like;
e. forming work networks in violence prevention efforts;
f. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
g. establishing a violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence; and
h. increasing public awareness in behaving in accordance with religious norms.
2. In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:

a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions;
d. health institutions;
e. social community institutions; and
f. business world and other institutions.

Article 10

1. The Regional Government is obliged to prepare prevention programs or activities as referred to in Article 9 paragraph (1) in the Regional Work Plan and Regional Strategic Plan;
2. Programs or activities as referred to in paragraph (1) to support the realization of the Regional Long-Term Development Plan and Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.

Article 11

1. Regional Apparatus that administers government affairs in the field of women's empowerment implements prevention as referred to in Article 9;
2. Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1) in carrying out prevention may cooperate with Vertical Agencies.

Article 12

1. Regional Government implements integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 5 letter b through activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. social rehabilitation services;
f. legal aid and assistance services; and/or
g. repatriation and reintegration.
2. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within police units;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
3. Implementation of services for victims of acts of violence is carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with P2TP2A with other social institutions;
4. P2TP2A and other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (3) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a networked manner;
5. Integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraphs (1) through (4) are implemented in accordance with standard operating procedures;
6. Provisions regarding Standard Operating Procedures as referred to in paragraph (5) are regulated by Governor's Regulation.

Article 14

1. Protection of children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in article 6 letter a is the responsibility of the Regional Government;
2. Protection of child victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by:
a. formulating policies for prevention, reduction of vulnerable risks, handling of victims and child data and information systems;
b. organizing maintenance, care, and social rehabilitation of neglected children, both within and outside institutions;
c. providing shelter, maintenance and care for neglected children; and organizing special protection for children.

Article 15

1. Provisions regarding prevention and services for acts of violence for the protection of women as referred to in Articles 7 through Article 13 apply mutatis mutandis to the protection of children from acts of violence;
2. In addition to conducting prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) also by means of increasing family capacity in child protection and parenting skills.

Article 16

(1) Special protection of children as referred to in Article 6 letter b is provided to:
a. Children in emergency situations;
b. Children in conflict with the law;
c. Children from minority and isolated groups;
d. Children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. Children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. Children who are victims of pornography;
g. Children with HIV/AIDS;
h. Child victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. Child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. Child victims of sexual crimes;
k. Child victims of terrorism networks;
l. Children with Disabilities;
m. Child victims of abuse and neglect;
n. Children with deviant social behavior; and
o. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their Parents' condition.
2. Regional Government in implementing handling of special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 17

1. Regional Government conducts cooperation and coordination in order to ensure the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence;
2. Cooperation and coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by Regional Apparatus that administers government affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 18

1. Regional Government in conducting cooperation and coordination on Protection of Women and children from acts of violence, establishes FP2ATK;
2. FP2ATK membership consists of elements of:
a. regional government;
b. vertical agencies;
c. educational institutions; and
d. community organizations concerned with the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
3. Organizational Structure of FP2ATK as referred to in paragraph (2) consists of:
a. policy formulation working group;
b. socialization working group;
c. service working group; and
d. data, information, monitoring and evaluation working group.
(4) Establishment and term of office of FP2ATK members as referred to in paragraph (1), is stipulated by Regent Decree.

Article 19

1. FP2ATK has the duties to:
a. provide input in policy formulation;
b. coordinate with Regional Apparatus and other social institutions;
c. socialize laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. collect and develop data and information related to the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. conduct review, monitoring, evaluation, and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence; and
f. provide reports, suggestions, input, and considerations to the Regent.

Article 22

1. Regional Government in the context of services for victims of acts of violence, establishes P2TP2A;
2. P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the duties to:
a. provide services, including:
1. receive complaints/reports of violence against women and/or children, consultation, and counseling;
2. receive and send case referrals from Regency/City Regional Government or institutions established by Regency/City Regional Government;
3. provide legal assistance and support;
4. health;
5. social rehabilitation;
6. legal services; and
7. repatriation and social reintegration.
b. provide protection to victims of acts of violence, reporters and witnesses.
3. Appointment of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) is stipulated by Regent Decree.
4. Regional Apparatus conducts guidance for P2TP2A in carrying out duties as referred to in paragraph (2).

Article 23

1. In certain circumstances the Regional Government may cooperate with: a. other regional governments;
b. the police;
c. advocates;
d. health workers;
e. social workers;
f. volunteer companions;
g. spiritual advisers;
h. other law enforcement officers, to assist victims in the trial/court session process;
i. the National Commission on Violence Against Women;
j. the Indonesian Child Protection Commission or a Child Protection Institution; and/or
k. certain parties desired for the benefit of the victim.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of: a. dissemination/outreach;
b. provision of legal aid;
c. health services;
d. victim assistance;
e. counseling;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. spiritual guidance; and/or
h. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 24

1. The Regent coordinates the protection of women and children from acts of violence with: a. the government;
b. Regency/City regional governments;
c. vertical agencies;
d. educational institutions; and
e. community institutions.
2. Coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. religious affairs;
b. health affairs;
c. education affairs;
d. social affairs;
e. legal affairs;
f. labor affairs; and
g. other affairs.

CHAPTER VII

COMMUNITY AND BUSINESS PARTICIPATION

Article 25

1. Every person may participate in providing protection to women and children from acts of violence;
2. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by: a. reporting acts of violence against women and/or children;
b. providing first aid to victims of Acts of Violence;
c. independently disseminating/outreaching the rights of women and children; and/or
d. establishing independent community social institutions.

CHAPTER X

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 29

1. The Regional Government carries out guidance and supervision of FP2ATK and other social institutions;
2. Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by the Regional Apparatus implementing women and child empowerment affairs;
3. The results of the implementation of guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (2) must be reported to the regional government.
